    <section aria-labelledby="m-rig">
      <div class="sechead"><h2 id="m-rig">Publisher rig</h2><p>Rebuilt in Blender on Sat 14 Nov, approved and exported to Art/Models/boss_fixed (import notes: README.md there).</p></div>

      <div class="how">
        <p><strong>Approved on Sat 14 Nov</strong> with one fix: the lapel that stuck out in the idle. Shaded creases of the collar had been painted as head; they now stay on the chest, and layers that lie on each other (lapel, jacket edge, tie) share their weights. The kung fu punch is kept whole; hit, knock-down and punch travel on the root bone.</p>
        <p><strong>Round 3, Sat 14 Nov:</strong> the tie has its own two bones: it hangs straight from the knot, lies on the belly, follows gravity sideways and lags a little. In the slam the raised hand turns its palm forward and stays in line with the forearm. The skeleton grew by two bones, so the mesh has to be imported again.</p>
        <p><strong>Round 4, Sat 14 Nov:</strong> in the wind-up of the slam the raised right hand was still bent outward at the cuff. The raised arm is now one straight line from shoulder to fingertips: the elbow hidden in the sleeve gives up its bend and the hand follows the same line. The tie was modelled into the shirt and pulled black spikes of it along when it swung away: it is now a free two-sided strip over a closed black shirt, so the mesh has to be imported once more (same skeleton).</p>
        <p><strong>Round 8, Sun 15 Nov:</strong> the neck and the knot. Skin spilled onto the collar under the chin and black onto the jowls: the border is now cut into the mesh along a smooth line, with plain black collar next to it. The top of the tie knot was dark with a dot of skin: the whole knot is gold. Mesh and base colour changed, so SK_Publisher.fbx has to be imported again with its textures (same skeleton, same clips). Leftover: with the head tilted far, as in the hit reaction, the border behind the ears is still uneven.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="models">
        <article class="model">
          <a class="sheet" href="rig/weights_after.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="rig/weights_after.jpg" alt="The new skin weights as a colour map: belly, chest and jacket on the spine, arms starting at the sleeves"></a>
          <div class="stage">
            <model-viewer data-glb="models/boss_fixed.glb.txt" alt="The Publisher on the rebuilt rig, playing a reworked clip" camera-controls auto-rotate autoplay shadow-intensity="0.6" exposure="1.1" camera-orbit="20deg 80deg auto" loading="lazy"></model-viewer>
            <div class="info">
              <div class="tag">The Publisher, rebuilt rig <span class="state">round 8</span><span class="code">boss_fixed</span></div>
              <div class="controls">
                <label class="code" for="clips-rig">clip</label>
                <select id="clips-rig" class="clips" aria-label="Rebuilt rig clip"></select>
              </div>
              <dl class="stats">
                <div><dt>bones</dt><dd>29: 22 Meshy names + root + 6 helpers</dd></div>
                <div><dt>helpers</dt><dd>belly, jacket_front_l, jacket_front_r, jacket_back, tie_01, tie_02</dd></div>
                <div><dt>influences</dt><dd>4 at most, normalised</dd></div>
                <div><dt>clips</dt><dd>the 7 Meshy clips, same names, + T_Pose</dd></div>
              </dl>
              <p>The Meshy model with the tie cut free, the neck border cut clean and the knot repainted. Turn it and pick a clip: the FBX files are exported from this file.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="grid wide">
        <figure>
          <a href="rig/weights_before.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="rig/weights_before.jpg" loading="lazy" alt="The Meshy rig at rest: the textured model and its weight colour map, where the clavicles and the head own most of the jacket"></a>
          <figcaption><div class="tag">What was wrong <span class="code">Meshy auto-rig</span></div><p>The shoulder joint sat about 25 cm too deep in the chest and the elbow at the armpit, so every arm move pulled the jacket. The jacket front was weighted to the clavicles and the head, the upper spine and the neck had almost no weight, and the thighs reached up into the belly.</p></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <a href="rig/weights_after.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="rig/weights_after.jpg" loading="lazy" alt="The rebuilt weights as a colour map"></a>
          <figcaption><div class="tag">What changed <span class="code">rebuilt</span></div><p>Joints moved to where the body bends (same bone names and axes, so the clips still fit). Weights painted from scratch: belly, chest and jacket follow the pelvis and the three spine bones, the head owns only the skin, arms start at the sleeves. A belly bone gives a small damped follow-through (5 cm at most), three jacket flap bones hang, lag and yield to the thighs. Every clip starts at the origin and stands on the ground.</p></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="sechead"><h2>Each clip, before and after</h2><p>Left: Meshy. Right: rebuilt. Click for full size.</p></div>
      <div class="grid wide">
      <figure>
        <a href="rig/Idle.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="rig/Idle.webp" loading="lazy" alt="Idle: the Meshy rig on the left, the rebuilt rig on the right, playing the same clip"></a>
        <figcaption><div class="tag">Idle <span class="code">Idle</span></div><p>Look-around idle in a side-on stance. Before: the lapels and tie are dragged by the head and the shoulders. After: the jacket stays on the chest, only the collar follows the neck.</p>
        <p class="code">121 frames, 4.0 s, loops · key frames: <a href="rig/keys_before_Idle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">before</a> / <a href="rig/keys_after_Idle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">after</a></p></figcaption>
